#### {{ sec.readthrough }} What a readthrough agent would produce at this stop codon

A nonsense allele is a readthrough target only if three things hold. Each is computed from this run's own data, and one of the three comes out against the strategy.

**Which stop codon.** The codon change is `{{ rt.codon_change }}`, so the premature stop is **{{ rt.stop_codon }}**, the {{ rt.stop_permissiveness }} of the three stop codons and the one ataluren was characterised against {{ cite('[@ng2021]') }}. The reference codon was re-read from the genome at `{{ rt.codon_region }}` and {{ 'matches the transcript annotation' if rt.reference_codon_confirmed else '**does not match the transcript annotation**, so the coordinates below are unreliable' }}.

**What 3′ context.** The base immediately after the stop exerts the strongest single-nucleotide influence on readthrough efficiency. Here it is **{{ rt.plus4_base_rna }}**, giving the context {{ rt.stop_context }}, which is position {{ rt.plus4_rank }} of {{ rt.plus4_of }} in the published order {{ rt.plus4_order }} {{ cite('[@mangkalaphiban2024]') }}. The codon favours readthrough and the local context works against it. Both are reported, so the weakness of the strategy stands beside its strength.

**What protein.** Readthrough does not restore the reference residue. Near-cognate decoding at {{ rt.stop_codon }} inserts Trp, Arg or Cys {{ cite('[@ng2021]') }}, so a readthrough agent would produce one of three specific full-length proteins rather than the wild-type one. AlphaMissense scores each of them, and Figure {{ fignum.readthrough_products }} places them among every other substitution the residue could carry:

| Readthrough product | AlphaMissense | Class |
|---|---|---|
{% for p in rt.readthrough_products %}
| {{ p.label }} | {{ p.alphamissense }} | {{ p.class }} |
{% endfor %}

{{ figure('readthrough_products') }}

Verdict: **{{ rt.verdict }}**. The residue is tolerant more broadly, which is the stronger claim: of the {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_substitutions_scored }} substitutions AlphaMissense scores at this position, {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_benign }} are benign, {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_ambiguous }} ambiguous and {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_pathogenic }} pathogenic, with a mean of {{ rt.residue_tolerance.mean_score }} against {{ rt.regional_means['whole protein'] }} for the protein as a whole. The position sits in the {{ rt.residue_region }}, not inside a folded domain. So the predicted readthrough product is a full-length protein carrying a tolerated substitution at a tolerant position between two domains.

**Why the combination, not readthrough alone.** The premature stop lies {{ rt.nmd_distance_to_last_junction_nt }} nt upstream of the last exon–exon junction, in exon {{ rt.ptc_exon }}, and is therefore {{ rt.nmd_verdict }}. That is the reason to pair a readthrough agent with nonsense-mediated-decay inhibition, not an argument against the axis: most of the transcript is degraded before it is translated, so a readthrough agent acts on a depleted substrate. The pharmacology is the weak point. No NMD inhibitor is approved; amlexanox is the only approved compound with NMD-inhibition literature and that literature is thin {{ cite('[@atanasova2017]') }}{{ cite('[@benslimane2023]') }}.

Whether the small percentage of full-length protein that readthrough typically restores would cross the phenotypic threshold is unknown, and the conditional framing in the residual-function paragraph above applies unchanged {{ cite('[@simmons2019]') }}.
{% endif %}
