{% if consanguinity_excluded %}
**Recurrent parental pregnancy loss is consistent with biallelic lethality.** Recurrent spontaneous abortion (HP:0200067) is among the released features. Runs of homozygosity give F_ROH(>=1.5 Mb) = {{ roh_f }}, so the parents are not detectably related, and each pregnancy of two unrelated carriers has a 25% chance of being biallelic. Complete knockout in mouse is embryonic-lethal with complete penetrance, between implantation and placentation. If biallelic loss is lethal in humans too, recurrent early pregnancy loss is the predicted consequence, and it is what the family history records. This is independent human evidence, from this child's own family, for the premise the whole null-plus-hypomorph argument rests on. It is consistent with that premise rather than proof of it: recurrent miscarriage is common and has many causes, and no products of conception were genotyped.
{% endif %}

**Residual function: a conditional inference.** {{ cite('[@+simmons2019]') }} report that nearly complete loss of BubR1 is required for microcephaly. Microcephaly is not among the features released to participants. **If** it is genuinely absent rather than unreported or unassessed, that would imply the missense allele retains meaningful activity, and strategies acting on residual protein would be acting on a partially functional system. The antecedent is not established here and is not asserted, under the feature-absence rule. Two further cautions apply — microcephaly is reported in 87% of MVA1 cases but is explicitly not required for the diagnosis {{ cite('[@callier2005]') }}, and this child is young, so a head-circumference trajectory may not be complete. The inference is recorded because it would matter if resolved, not because it is resolved.

### {{ sec.problem }} The therapeutic problem, and why the strategy is repurposing

{% if tract and tract.found %}
**The causal gene cannot be drugged directly.** Open Targets records {{ tract.gene }} as {% if tract.is_essential %}an **essential** gene{% else %}not essential{% endif %}, and no modality has reached the clinic against it:

| Modality | Tractability evidence present | Absent |
|---|---|---|
{% for name, m in tract_mod %}
| {{ name }} | {{ m.positive | join(', ') if m.positive else '**none**' }} | {{ m.negative | join(', ') | mdcell }} |
{% endfor %}

There is no approved drug, no advanced clinical candidate and no Phase 1 candidate against {{ tract.gene }} in any modality. Small-molecule tractability is not zero — {{ tract.gene }} is flagged as a druggable family with a high-quality ligand — but it has no structure with a ligand and no high- or medium-quality pocket recorded, and nothing has been carried forward. The one modality with positive evidence beyond ligandability is the **degrader** axis, and that points the wrong way: this disease is loss of protein function, so a molecule that removes the remaining protein is the opposite of what is wanted. Direct targeting is therefore off the table. Every candidate axis below acts on something other than {{ tract.gene }}: restoring protein from the nonsense allele, or reducing the downstream cost of the aneuploidy that its loss produces.
{% endif %}

**The drugged neighbourhood runs the same way.** Every drugged node around BubR1 in the mitotic machinery — PLK1, AURKB, CDK1, TTK, KIF11, TOP2A, CENP-E, BIRC5 — is the target of **inhibitors**, developed to *reduce* mitotic fidelity in dividing tumour cells. This disorder is a loss of mitotic fidelity. Almost every drug that exists near this pathway would deepen the defect rather than compensate for it.

That asymmetry is the central problem of Track 2 for this gene, and it is why the pipeline encodes it as a rule rather than as a warning: *an inhibitor of a mitotic-fidelity gene is contraindicated in a mitotic-fidelity disorder.* Candidates are tested against it automatically, and the ones it rejects are listed below rather than dropped silently.

**So the strategy is compensation, not replacement.** No available molecule restores BubR1 kinase activity. What a repurposing hypothesis can do is act on something *other than* the broken protein so that the consequences of its loss are reduced. Each candidate axis is one such mechanism, and each is a hypothesis about a different point of leverage:

- restore some full-length protein from the nonsense allele (readthrough, NMD inhibition) — **populated as the readthrough axis below**, and characterised in section {{ sec.readthrough }};
- stabilise the partially functional protein the missense allele already makes;
- reduce the cellular cost of the aneuploidy that results (proteostasis and chaperone support) — **populated as the proteostasis axis below**;
- treat downstream consequences that are themselves druggable, where the spindle checkpoint is not.

The candidate list below is organised by that logic, and by how well each axis is supported for this child rather than for the syndrome in general.

