#### What the mechanism implies for the therapy already given

This is a mechanism finding, not a repurposing candidate, and it bears on treatment the child is already receiving. It is a consideration for the treating team. It is **not** a recommendation to alter a rhabdomyosarcoma protocol, and nothing here licenses withdrawing therapy given with curative intent.

**The vincristine asymmetry.** Vincristine kills by disrupting the spindle so that the spindle assembly checkpoint holds the cell in mitosis until it dies there. A cell with a weakened checkpoint does not arrest; it slips through mitosis and survives. That is a documented resistance mechanism for antimitotic drugs: checkpoint-compromised cells are less sensitive to paclitaxel {{ cite('[@sudo2004]') }} and to microtubule-targeted drugs generally {{ cite('[@lee2004]') }}, mitotic slippage is the established route to that resistance {{ cite('[@cheng2017]') }}, and the one report that is specific to this protein runs the same way in reverse: BubR1 is repressed in acute myeloid leukaemia, and restoring it re-sensitises the cells to antimitotic therapy {{ cite('[@schnerch2013]') }}.

This child's checkpoint defect is germline, so it is present in the tumour as well as in normal tissue. Two consequences follow, both stated as hypotheses:

1. **Efficacy.** The tumour may be intrinsically less sensitive to vincristine's cytotoxic mechanism than a checkpoint-competent tumour of the same histology.
2. **Toxicity.** Vincristine's dose-limiting toxicity is peripheral neuropathy, which acts through axonal microtubule transport in post-mitotic neurons. That mechanism does not involve the checkpoint, so it is not reduced by the defect. This child is additionally a CYP3A5 non-expresser, the higher-neuropathy-risk genotype in the reports cited in the pharmacogenomic section.

For the same drug in the same child, plausibly reduced efficacy and plausibly undiminished or increased toxicity. That asymmetry is the finding.

**The DNA-damaging agents run the same way by a different route.** Cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, doxorubicin, etoposide and radiotherapy do not act through the checkpoint, so the slippage argument does not apply to them. The argument for caution there is the one already made in this report: a chromosomal-instability background plus mutagenic exposure raises the risk of a therapy-related second malignancy. The single published rhabdomyosarcoma in this syndrome was treated with reduced-intensity chemotherapy {{ cite('[@nishitaniisa2019]') }}. That paper's full text was not accessible to this analysis, so it is cited for the approach its title states and not for any regimen detail, which would have to be read from the paper before being built on.

**Limits of the analogy, and of the inference.** De-intensification protocols in ataxia telangiectasia, Nijmegen breakage syndrome and Fanconi anaemia rest on DNA-repair defects; this is a segregation defect, so the analogy is suggestive rather than direct. The antimitotic-resistance literature comes from cancer cell lines with acquired checkpoint loss, not from germline hypomorphs. The tumour's own checkpoint status is unknown, because no tumour sequencing was released, and a somatic event could have moved it in either direction. The anti-target rule governs new proposals only and is not applied to treatment already given.

#### Why the tumour-directed candidates are all tier 3: the tumour genotype is missing

All five tumour-directed candidates share a single blocker, and it is the same one each time. The released data is germline whole-genome sequencing. Precision-oncology candidates are selected on the tumour's genotype — fusion status, copy number, homologous-recombination status, aneuploidy score — none of which is derivable from germline data. The candidates cannot be assessed, and the reason does not differ between them.

| Candidate | Tumour data that would make it assessable |
|---|---|
| abemaciclib | histology; PAX3/PAX7-FOXO1 fusion status; CDK4 copy number or expression |
| olaparib | homologous-recombination deficiency or aneuploidy score; HR-pathway variant status |
| niraparib | as olaparib, plus any rhabdomyosarcoma-specific PARP data, of which none was found |
| ceralasertib | ATM status; replication-stress markers |
| simvastatin | none — the proposed mechanism is unsupported; retained as a worked negative |

The table above is in effect a test-request list. If a tumour pathology record exists for this child, these are the specific results that would make each candidate assessable.

**What this says about the substrate.** Germline whole-genome sequencing is the right data for mechanism-directed candidates and for pharmacogenomics, and the wrong data for tumour-directed ones. The data released for this challenge therefore points toward mechanism, which is where the readthrough axis, the proteostasis axis and the vincristine finding sit, and away from the tumour-directed candidates. That is the reason this report's weight rests where it does.

